                "senderName": "NWS Fort Worth TX",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued October 1 at 10:17AM CDT until October 1 at 12:30PM CDT by NWS Fort Worth TX",
                "description": "FFWFWD\n\nThe National Weather Service in Fort Worth has extended the\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nNorthwestern Dallas County in north central Texas...\nDenton County in north central Texas...\nTarrant County in north central Texas...\n\n* Until 1230 PM CDT.\n\n* At 1017 AM CDT, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nthunderstorms producing heavy rain across the warned area. Up to 4\ninches of rain have fallen. Additional rainfall amounts up to 2\ninches are possible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing\nor expected to begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar and automated gauges.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nFort Worth, Arlington, Irving, Grand Prairie, Carrollton, Frisco,\nDenton, Lewisville, Flower Mound, Mansfield, Euless, Bedford,\nGrapevine, Haltom City, Keller, Coppell and Hurst.\n\nThis includes the following highways...\nInterstate 35E between mile markers 437 and 466. Interstate 35W\nbetween mile markers 38 and 85. Interstate 35 between mile markers\n468 and 470. Interstate 20 between mile markers 423 and 456.\nInterstate 30 between mile markers 2 and 37.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nBe aware of your surroundings and do not drive on flooded roads.",
                "response": "Avoid",
